1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,800
Candy — LeetCode 135

2
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:05,600
Problem: children in a row, each with a rating. Give the fewest candies so everyone gets at least 1.

3
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:09,000
And a child with a higher rating than a neighbor must get more candy than that neighbor.

4
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:12,200
Example: ratings = [2,3,4,3,2,1] — index 2 is a peak, it must beat both sides.

5
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:16,600
Hint: one scan can't satisfy both neighbors at once, so use two greedy passes.

6
00:00:16,600 --> 00:00:21,600
Left to right fixes the up-slopes, right to left fixes the down-slopes, and each child keeps the larger.

7
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:24,500
Everyone starts with 1 candy.

8
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:28,000
Left pass: rating 3 beats rating 2, so candy becomes 2.

9
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:31,000
Rating 4 beats 3, candy becomes 3. The rest of the row isn't rising, so it stays at 1.

10
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Left pass done — the up-slope [1,2,3] is correct.

11
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Right pass: rating 2 beats 1, candy becomes 2; rating 3 beats 2, candy becomes 3.

12
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:41,500
At the peak, the long drop on the right forces max(3, 4) — candy bumps up to 4.

13
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:44,500
The left side isn't higher than the peak, so nothing else changes.

14
00:00:44,500 --> 00:00:48,500
Add them up: 1+2+4+3+2+1 = 13. That's the minimum.

15
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:52,000
Time O(n), space O(n) for the candy array.

16
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:56,500
